response:
Since $AB=AC$, $BC$ is the base of the isosceles triangle. We know that the altitude $AM$ bisects the base $BC$ so that $BM=CM.$ We want to make the left side $\cos 3x,$ so we divide out by two. This makes our expression look more like a double angle formula. <end think>
[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, M;

A = (0,11);
D = (0,1);
B = (-11,0);
C = (11,0);
M = (B + C)/2;

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--M);
draw(B--D--C);

label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, SW);
label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$D$", D, NW);
label("$M$", M, S);
[/asy]
Assistant: <think>Well now we have a right triangle with hypotenuse $AC=11$ and leg height $AD=10.$ If we let $x=MC,$ we know that $AC^2=AD^2+DC^2+AM^2$ by the Pythagorean Theorem. Expanding out to be quadratic form gives $x^2 -22x + 24 = 0.$ Therefore $x=2$ or $x=12,$ giving us $BC=14.$ We can split $\triangle ABC$ into two right triangles so that $\angle BAC=\angle C=M=\alpha.$ <end think>[/asy]
Assistant: <think有很多种 ways to solve this. We can either use the half-angle formula for $\cos 3\alpha$ or solve $\frac{\sqrt{BC^2 + 4DM^2 –2BC \cdot DM \cdot \cos 3\alpha}}{2\cdot AD} =\cos \alpha$ for values of $\alpha$ around $45$ degrees. <end think>[/asy]
Assistant: <though>Thus we have a right triangle with cos $\alpha$ equals two thirds. If the triangle has a hypotenuse (before before halving) of $11,$ then the opposite side would be of length $10,$ and the base length would be $6.$ Thus by half-angle formula after halving, the base length becomes $x=2\sqrt6$ and $AC=11$ by the double angle formula. <end think>[/asy]
Assistant: <think>. The perimeter is $21+2\sqrt6.$</end think>
